illustrates → Problem; involves → ST-01, ST-02, ST-04, ST-07, SA-01, SA-02, SA-04; reimagined-as → DS-01 (§2.1).

= Problem Scenario - Rain cancels the walk [PS-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|PS-03
|Name|Rain cancels the walk
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Constructed from SA-01, SA-02, SA-03 and the a2.1 stakeholder records; literature and group reasoning only. No resident or care worker has confirmed it.

* Forbes et al. (2015): exercise may improve ADL ability in dementia
* Anderson-Hanley et al. (2012): cycling with a virtual route keeps attention better than plain cycling (older adults, not dementia-specific)
)))
|Narrative|(((
It rains for a week in November, and the daily walks are cancelled. The resident uses a wheelchair. A care worker wheels them to the pedal exerciser in the common room and starts it. After three minutes the resident stops pedalling and asks where they are going. The care worker is called away, and the exerciser is not used again that week. In the evenings the resident is restless.
)))
|Stakeholders involved|(((
* ST-01: Resident with dementia
* ST-02: Care worker
* ST-06: Physiotherapist
)))
|Activities involved|(((
* SA-01: Accompanied walk
* SA-02: Fitting walks between care tasks
* SA-03: Bad-weather indoor exercise
)))
|Breakdown point|Indoor exercise without destination or company does not keep the resident's attention
|Causes|(((
Root causes:

* Exercise without destination: no route, nothing to see
* The walk's company and conversation are missing
* Only the exercise is replaced; not the daylight, sights, sounds or calm

Contributing causes:

* Staff needed to set up and start each session
* Shared device, occupied or unfamiliar
* Short, dark winter days limit outdoor walks further
)))
|Sub-problems|(((
1. Substitution: nothing replaces the cancelled walk
1. Engagement: resident stops after a few minutes
1. Calm: no quiet, attentive stand-in for the walk
1. Set-up: each session needs a care worker to start it
1. Accessibility: must work from a wheelchair
)))
|Out of scope|(((
* Prescribing exercise intensity - ST-06
* Walking outside in unsafe weather (ice, storm)
* Building changes such as a covered walkway - ST-05
)))

== Graph edges ==

illustrates → Problem; involves → ST-01, ST-02, ST-06, SA-01, SA-02, SA-03; reimagined-as → DS-02 (§2.1).

= Problem Scenario - Lost in the recording [PS-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|PS-04
|Name|Lost in the recording
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Constructed from SA-05 and the a2.1 stakeholder records; literature and group reasoning only. No resident or activity coordinator has confirmed it.

* Churcher Clarke et al. (2017): mindfulness for people with dementia needs adapting - shorter, more concrete, more repetition
)))
|Narrative|(((
Wednesday afternoon, relaxation session in the snoezel room. Six residents in recliners. The activity coordinator plays a recorded exercise: "Imagine you are walking on a beach. Let your thoughts drift away." The resident frowns: "Which beach? Where are we going?" The recording is already on the breath; the resident is still on the beach. They try to get up.

The coordinator is tucking a blanket around another resident. When they turn round, the resident is at the door, asking for their coat.
)))
|Stakeholders involved|(((
* ST-01: Resident with dementia
* ST-08: Activity coordinator
)))
|Activities involved|SA-05: Relaxation session
|Breakdown point|Guidance runs at its own pace, in abstract language; nobody notices the drift
|Causes|(((
Root causes:

* Abstract, imaginative instructions need working memory and imagination
* Fixed recording: no pause, no adjustment to the listener
* One leader for many residents

Contributing causes:

* Hearing loss
* Dim, unfamiliar room can upset
)))
|Sub-problems|(((
1. Comprehension: wording too abstract
1. Pacing: fixed tempo
1. Re-anchoring: drift goes unnoticed and unanswered
1. Lasting effect: calm ends with the session
)))
|Out of scope|(((
* Treating anxiety disorders - ST-06
* Designing the home's activity programme - ST-08, ST-06
)))

== Graph edges ==

illustrates → Problem; involves → ST-01, ST-08, SA-05; reimagined-as → DS-02 (§2.1).

= Problem Scenario - Restless at dusk [PS-05] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|PS-05
|Name|Restless at dusk
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Constructed from SA-06, SA-07 and the a2.1 stakeholder records; literature and group reasoning only. No resident or care worker has confirmed it.

* Khachiyants et al. (2011): sundowning
* Ancoli-Israel et al. (2003): daylight and sleep in Alzheimer's disease
* Schneider et al. (2005): antipsychotic risk in dementia
)))
|Narrative|(((
It has rained all day; the resident has not been outside. At five they start pacing the corridor with their coat on: "My mother is waiting." Dinner is being served and the evening shift is arriving.

A care worker walks them back to their room and switches on the radio - the news, about a flood - and is called away. Twenty minutes later the resident is at the exit door. The night nurse gives the as-needed tablet.
)))
|Stakeholders involved|(((
* ST-01: Resident with dementia
* ST-02: Care worker
* ST-06: Nurse, physician (PRN prescription)
)))
|Activities involved|(((
* SA-06: Winding down to rest
* SA-07: Listening to radio, news or audiobooks
)))
|Breakdown point|Restlessness peaks when staff time is lowest; calming down needs a person who is not there
|Causes|(((
Root causes:

* Sundowning
* No walk, no daylight that day

Contributing causes:

* Dinner and shift handover
* Distressing news at the wrong hour
* Medication as the quickest answer
)))
|Sub-problems|(((
1. Prevention: no walk, so a restless evening
1. Calming: no one to stay
1. Content: distressing audio
1. Escalation: medication as default
)))
|Out of scope|(((
* Prescribing or withholding medication - ST-06
* Physical restraint
* Monitoring sleep or night-time movement in the bedroom
)))

== Graph edges ==

illustrates → Problem; involves → ST-01, ST-02, ST-06, SA-06, SA-07; reimagined-as → DS-03 (§2.1).

= Problem Scenario - Radio on, nobody listening [PS-06] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|PS-06
|Name|Radio on, nobody listening
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Constructed from SA-07 and the a2.1 stakeholder records; literature and group reasoning only. No resident or family member has confirmed it.

* Gerdner (2000): individualised music outperformed generic "relaxation" music on agitation
)))
|Narrative|(((
The resident loved brass band music and football, and read the morning paper front to back. In the care home, the radio in their room is tuned to a pop station; a care worker switched it on months ago. The resident cannot work the buttons.

On Sunday their daughter puts on an audiobook - chapter seven of a detective story. The resident asks who all these people are, then pulls the plug. The daughter's list of favourite programmes is in a drawer.
)))
|Stakeholders involved|(((
* ST-01: Resident with dementia
* ST-02: Care worker
* ST-03: Family member
* ST-08: Activity coordinator
)))
|Activities involved|SA-07: Listening to radio, news or audiobooks
|Breakdown point|What plays is chosen by whoever was there last, not by the resident; content fits neither the person, the hour nor the memory span
|Causes|(((
Root causes:

* Devices too complex to operate
* Preferences held by family, not by whoever switches on
* Audiobooks and serials assume memory across days

Contributing causes:

* No staff time to ask
* News not adapted to the listener
* Hearing loss
)))
|Sub-problems|(((
1. Choice: resident cannot select
1. Fit: content not matched to the person
1. Timing: hour and mood ignored (news at night)
1. Continuity: plot lost between sessions
1. Overload: always on
)))
|Out of scope|(((
* Producing content
* Hiding news from the resident - choose what to offer first, never what may be heard
* Television
)))

